/**
 * Exporting Camel programs as ONNX models: `export_model` writes the model of a function applied
 * to an example argument, and `supported_operators` lists the operator URIs the backend can
 * lower. The module is the ONNX exporter, not a runtime: loading it and asking what it can lower
 * needs neither the ONNX runtime nor a GPU; only a run of `export_model` writes a file.
 */

// The declaration of the one fixed signature of the C++ module `modules/onnx` (design
// principles 6.4). This file is the one source of
// `supported_operators`'s signature; the C++ side keeps the kernel, the executor and the purity.
//
// `export_model` stays in C++ and is recorded as a computed signature in
// `stdlib/signatures/onnx.cml`: its first parameter is a function of any parameter list and
// result (`(...) => R`), its `example` parameter is a tensor, a tuple or a struct whose leaves
// have to be the function's parameter tree (a predicate on the type variable), and its last two
// parameters are optional, so its arity is three to five. None of that is expressible in a
// declaration, and a declaration that wrote one instance of it would accept calls the resolver
// refuses.
//
// `supported_operators` is registered with effects until someone has checked that it is not
// effectful; the declaration keeps that purity (a declaration carries types, not effects).


/// Lists the operator URIs the ONNX backend can lower at its default opset, in the order the
/// lowering table reports them. A capability report only: it reads the table compiled into the
/// module and never touches the file system or the ONNX runtime.
/// @effect reports the lowering table of the backend
/// @example let uris = supported_operators()
export func supported_operators(): string[]

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Passes: functions the pipeline applies to the program
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Writes the model of the program's `main` (or of the one `export_model` call it makes) as an
/// ONNX file, translating the function `main` refers to itself. `out` overrides the path
/// argument of `export_model` (then exactly one call is allowed) and names the file when the
/// program makes no request.
with <out: string = "">
export func export_model::<F>(f: F): F! when is_function::<F>()
